cution; no dividend policy mentioned","src":"concall","as_of":"H1FY26"},"management_tone":{"title":"Management quality / tone","body":"Highly confident, forward-looking, and execution-focused; clear roadmap for capacity doubling, EPC entry, and pan-India expansion; transparent on capex needs and funding strategy; emphasis on operational discipline and market share capture","src":"concall","as_of":"H1FY26"},"risks":{"title":"Risks & watch-items","body":"EPC competition from KEC, Polycab, Calcutta-based players; execution risk in new Dankuni ramp-up; working capital pressure from EPC projects; raw material price spikes beyond PV clause coverage; delayed government payments (minor, 10-20 days)","src":"concall","as_of":"H1FY26"},"non_obvious":{"title":"Non-obvious takeaways","body":"EPC division led by ex-30-year veteran Rajesh Jhunjhunwala signals serious intent; vendor approvals in Punjab/Himachal are not just geographic but strategic to avoid losing bids due to lack of eligibility; Rs. 286 Cr. order book includes legacy orders, but new EPC pipeline is the real growth lever","src":"concall","as_of":"H1FY26"},"challenges":{"title":"Challenges → beneficiaries & losers","body":"| Challenge | Beneficiary/Loser | Dir | Conviction | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EPC expansion exposes JD Cables to capital-intensive, low-margin competition | Laser Cables | loser | Medium | Smaller regional EPC player with limited capacity; JD's vertical integration and IPO-funded scale will erode its tender competitiveness |\n| Rising demand for transmission-distribution cables from RDSS and grid projects | Power Grid Corporation of India | beneficiary | High | JD Cables' vendor approvals and capacity expansion directly enable it to capture more RDSS-related cable supply contracts |","src":"concall","as_of":"H1FY26"}}}